{
  "narrative_body": "Zelensky this week accused the Trump administration of being \"played\" by Russia — and the same week secured over $5 billion in European military commitments, with Washington neither present nor paying.\n\nThe transformation in European security architecture was visible at the 34th Ukraine Defence Contact Group meeting in Berlin on April 15, co-chaired by the UK and Germany. The UK pledged its largest-ever drone package: 120,000 drones worth £752 million. Germany committed a €4 billion package including Patriot missiles. Belgium and Spain pledged €1 billion between them. Norway signed a drone cooperation declaration and committed an additional €560 million for frontline drone support. The total came to $4 billion for air defense and $1.5 billion for drones — all European, none American. What began as gap-filling after US support wavered has become a distinct architecture. Zelensky used the forum to push allies toward a European-wide ballistic missile defense system, framed not as a Ukrainian request but as a collective European security requirement. He visited Berlin, Oslo, Rome, and The Hague over four days, wrapping bilateral defense-industrial agreements around the multilateral pledges.\n\nThe break with Washington was equally visible. The Trump administration extended for the fourth time a waiver allowing Russian oil to be sold despite Western sanctions, citing disruption from the Iran war. Zelensky identified more than 110 shadow fleet tankers carrying 12 million tonnes of oil — anticipated proceeds of roughly $10 billion for Moscow's war budget. He said the waiver \"sustains Russia's illusion the war can continue\" and that Russia had \"played the Americans again — played the president of the United States.\" The statement, amplified in English on X, crossed a rhetorical threshold Ukraine had avoided throughout the Trump administration's return. It sat alongside a report that US envoys Steve Witkoff and Jared Kushner had visited Moscow three times without visiting Kyiv once. Zelensky said he was ready to receive them \"even tomorrow.\" The US peace channel has moved toward Moscow, not between the parties.\n\nEven as Zelensky toured European capitals, Ukraine's drone forces were demonstrating why Russia is alarmed. Strikes hit refineries in the Samara region, an oil depot in Sevastopol, Lukoil's terminal at the Vysotsk Baltic Sea port, and Caspian drilling platforms. Drone forces commander Robert Brovdi said strikes on four Russian export terminals had cut daily oil shipments by approximately 880,000 barrels — a Ukrainian military claim that Reuters could not independently verify at publication. Zelensky put March oil revenue losses at $2.3 billion; Oleksandr Syrskyi, the commander-in-chief, reported 76 Russian industrial targets struck in March. The SBU separately struck two landing ships and a warship in Crimea. Russia's response to the campaign crossed a new threshold: the defence ministry published the addresses of European companies involved in joint weapons production with Ukraine. Dmitry Medvedev characterized the list as \"potential targets for Russian armed forces.\" It was the first time Russia had explicitly threatened European civilian industrial infrastructure by name — a development that raises direct questions about NATO Article 5 and sharpens the stakes of every defense-industrial agreement signed at Ramstein.\n\nRussia's aerial response was the deadliest of the year. Over 24 hours from April 15, Russia launched 659 drones and 44 ballistic and cruise missiles against Kyiv, Odesa, Dnipro, and Zaporizhzhia. At least 18 people died, including a 12-year-old child. In Odesa, eight were killed when a drone hit a residential building and a ballistic missile then struck the same location. The attack came after the expiration of a 32-hour Easter ceasefire Putin had declared the week before. Andrii Sybiha, the foreign minister, called the strikes \"war crimes that must be stopped.\"\n\nThe week's other shock came inside Kyiv itself. On April 18, Dmytro Vasylchenkov — a 58-year-old Ukrainian citizen born in Moscow — opened fire on pedestrians in the Holosiivskyi district, killed four on the street, and took hostages in a Velmart supermarket. He killed one hostage during a 40-minute standoff before police shot him dead. A woman wounded on the street died later; 14 more were injured, including a 12-year-old boy. The SBU opened a terrorism investigation. The Guardian reported the attacker had Russian bank accounts until at least 2021, a Russian phone number, traveled to Russia in 2016, and had posted content denying Ukraine's right to exist. Kremlin operatives have recruited more than 800 Ukrainians in the past two years for attacks on infrastructure and draft offices. The following day, the head of Ukraine's patrol police resigned after two officers were accused of fleeing the scene; Zelensky demanded a full review of patrol police conduct.\n\nBehind the week's headline events, government petitions registered accumulated wartime grievances. One, requesting criminal penalties for promoting anti-Ukrainian narratives under existing law, crossed the 25,000-signature threshold requiring a mandatory Cabinet response. A second came from 2022 volunteers who say they cannot leave — they enlisted willingly but now face indefinite service with no defined endpoint. A third requested a moratorium on rural school closures, arguing that in some communities children must travel more than 20 kilometres under active air alerts with no guaranteed shelter. None of these will produce policy this week. Together they map what Ukrainians are living with.",
  "other_stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Russia launches year's deadliest aerial strike on Ukraine — ~700 drones, 17-18 killed across multiple cities",
      "summary": "Russia launched its most destructive aerial attack of the year overnight on April 15-16, sending approximately 700 drones and dozens of ballistic and cruise missiles against Ukrainian cities, killing at least 17-18 people including a 12-year-old child. Zelensky called Putin a 'global threat' comparable to Nazis while speaking in the Netherlands, and demanded more international pressure on Moscow."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Zelensky's European tour, Ramstein meeting yield $5B+ in defense pledges: 4B for air defense, $1.5B for drones, 120,000 UK drones",
      "summary": "President Zelensky visited Berlin, Oslo, Rome, and The Hague over April 14-17, securing major military aid and defense industry agreements. The 34th Ramstein Ukraine Defence Contact Group meeting in Berlin (April 15), co-chaired by UK and Germany with Defence Minister Fedorov, yielded $4 billion in air defense commitments and $1.5 billion in drone funding. The UK announced its largest-ever drone package (120,000 drones); Germany committed a €4B defense package including Patriot missiles; Belgium and Spain pledged €1 billion; Norway and Ukraine signed a drone cooperation declaration. Zelensky also pushed for a European ballistic missile defense system."
    }
  ]
}

